  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how elevated atmospheric CO2 affects methane exchange in wetland versus upland soils through plant and root processes. In both settings, higher CO2 appears to stimulate photosynthesis, sending more photosynthate or root exudates belowground and increasing fine-root decomposition. In wetlands, where soils are waterlogged, this added carbon input is shown driving stronger CH4 emission from the soil. The diagram also notes that improved water-use efficiency is less important there, implying that the main effect is greater carbon supply to anaerobic soil processes.\n\nIn upland soils, the response is more mixed. Elevated CO2 again increases photosynthesis and belowground carbon inputs, but it also reduces evapotranspiration at the leaf level, shown by changes in stomata, which leads to increased soil moisture. Under ambient CO2, the upland soil mainly shows CH4 uptake, but under elevated CO2 both CH4 uptake and some CH4 emission are indicated. Overall, the figure suggests that elevated CO2 can enhance methane release in wetlands directly through increased plant-derived carbon, while in uplands it alters the balance between methane consumption and production by both increasing root inputs and making soils wetter.",
